        <section id="about" class="section-pad"><div class="container"><div class="row align-items-center g-5"><div class="col-lg-6"><div class="section-kicker">Vanakkam · வணக்கம்</div><h2 class="section-title">A temple shaped by devotion, community and time.</h2><p class="body-copy">Sri Selva Vinayakar Koyil began with Tamil families gathering for prayer in Brisbane. Their shared devotion grew into a traditional temple at South Maclean, where worship, education and community life continue today.</p><p class="body-copy">From the first congregational prayers to the 2015 Maha Kumbabishekam, this is a story held by generations of devotees.</p><div class="quote mt-4">விக்னங்களை தீர்ப்பவர் விநாயகர்</div><button class="btn btn-outline-temple mt-4" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#fullTempleHistory" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="fullTempleHistory">Read the full history <i class="bi bi-chevron-down ms-2"></i></button></div><div class="col-lg-6"><div class="image-frame story-image"></div><div class="row g-2 mt-2"><div class="col-6"><div class="small text-muted">The first temple</div><div class="small">Early years of worship at South Maclean</div></div><div class="col-6"><div class="small text-muted">The temple today</div><div class="small">A place for worship and Tamil culture</div></div></div></div></div><div class="collapse mt-5" id="fullTempleHistory"><div class="row g-4"><div class="col-lg-7"><div class="p-4 bg-white border rounded-3"><div class="section-kicker">Our history</div><h3 class="h2 mt-2 mb-4">From congregational prayer to a living koyil.</h3><div class="history-copy"><p class="body-copy">The migration of Tamils to Queensland began only recently. Before 1985, only a few Tamil families lived in the state. In 1983, Hindu families began monthly congregational prayer meetings, first in homes and then at the SWARA Hall in Spring Hill. Regular prayer meetings have continued there since that time.</p><p class="body-copy">As the congregation grew, a traditional Hindu temple in Brisbane became necessary. A Convening Committee was formed on 25 April 1986 and registered as Hindu Ahlaya Sangam. The first Management Committee was elected unanimously on 12 October 1986, and subsequent committees have continued to be elected yearly.</p><p class="body-copy">From 1987 to 1990, the committees raised funds to purchase land. In 1990, 4.5 acres of elevated virgin bushland was secured at South Maclean, 35 kilometres south of Brisbane, near the Logan River.</p><p class="body-copy">Planning consent was sought in July 1991 and granted through the Planning and Environment Courts in May 1992. The foundation was laid on 10 June 1993. The Maha Mandapam was completed first, followed by the arrival of deities, shrine doors, stapathies and sculptors from South India.</p><p class="body-copy">Construction of the shrines commenced on 24 June 1994. The first Brisbane Selva Vinayakar Koyil was consecrated on 5 February 1995, witnessed by hundreds of devotees and well-wishers from across Australia.</p><p class="body-copy mb-0">Built in the traditional Indian style according to Silpa Sastra and Agamas, the temple includes the central Vinayakar shrine, Siva and Abirami, Maha Vishnu, Murukan, Navagraha and Vairavar. The temple was rebuilt at the same location and reopened with the Maha Kumbabishekam on 1 May 2015.</p></div></div></div><div class="col-lg-5"><img class="w-100 rounded-3 mb-3" style="height:220px;object-fit:cover" src="{{ asset('images/about/SELVA VINAYAHAR TEMPLE.jpg') }}" alt="Sri Selva Vinayakar Koyil in its early years"><img class="w-100 rounded-3" style="height:220px;object-fit:cover" src="{{ asset('images/about/ssvk.jpg') }}" alt="Sri Selva Vinayakar Koyil during construction"><div class="quote mt-3">The temple also serves as a centre for religious classes, music, dance, cultural programs and talks by scholars and religious leaders.</div></div></div></div></div></section>
